###### Abstract

In a multi-orbit (100 ks) _XMM-Newton_ exposure of the original black widow pulsar, PSR J1959+2048, we measure the strong orbital modulation caused by intrabinary shock (IBS) emission. The IBS light curve peak appears asymmetric, which we attribute to sweep-back effects in the companion wind. We also see evidence for an X-ray eclipse by the companion and its wind. Together with the IBS fit, this supports an edge-on i\\sim 90^{\\circ} view of the system and a modest \\sim 1.8M_{\\odot} mass for the recycled pulsar. Our IBS fit parameters imply a wind flux that, if persistent, would evaporated the companion within a few Gyr. pulsars: general -- pulsars: individual (PSR J1959+2048) +

